WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.239 --> 00:00:04.650
Narrator: To get a shot
like this from "Shang-Chi,"

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:06.627
Hollywood primes cars like this one

00:00:06.627 --> 00:00:09.610
to get crushed dramatically on camera.

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:11.092
Workman: Drop!

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.830
Narrator: And that work is
up to the team at JEM FX,

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:19.590
which sets cars up for optimal destruction

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:21.840
in movies like "Shang-Chi,"

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:24.165
"Taken 3,"

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:27.728
and "Ghostbusters: Afterlife."

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:31.350
We visited JEM to see how it designs cars

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:34.590
to get spectacularly destroyed on-screen

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:36.173
exactly as planned.

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:42.180
In real life, cars don't
always get destroyed

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:44.430
in the most cinematic ways.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:47.820
Even when hit repeatedly,

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.280
a car roof won't always cave in on itself

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:52.740
like we're used to seeing in movies.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:54.660
That's where scoring comes in --

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:57.120
stripping away the protective
sheet metal in a car

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.100
and gutting it of its support.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:01.267
Cody: We're going to start
by cutting this pillar

00:01:01.267 --> 00:01:04.733
from here to here to basically
weaken the structure.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:09.750
So, now that we've finished our cut,

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:12.810
we're going to take a
pry bar and simply remove

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:16.563
this small chunk of the front A pillar.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:19.500
Narrator: Each cut determines

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:22.050
where and how the car will fold.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:24.990
Cody: We're going to go through
and do some weakening cuts

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:27.270
to help this fold a little bit easier

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:29.040
under a little less weight.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.310
Narrator: They target the A,
B, and C pillars of the car.

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:33.570
Cody: Now, when you're doing this,

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:37.140
you have to ensure not to cut
the outside of the vehicle,

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:38.220
because then it'd be visible

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:40.530
and you could tell something's up with it.

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:43.350
So we like to try and
hide all of our stuff.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:45.870
Narrator: Then they put the
whole thing back together.

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:47.310
Cody: So, we're going to start out

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:50.280
by reconnecting our wire harness.

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:52.500
Then we'll take our interior cover,

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:56.580
put it back on, replace
our weather stripping.

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:58.680
And now we're back to the stock interior.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:00.570
It looks like a normal
car from the inside,

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:01.980
but it will crush.

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:04.440
Narrator: It took four
hours to score the car,

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:06.300
all so it could be destroyed in seconds

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:08.250
by a 3,000-pound weight.

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:09.720
And the areas they scored

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:11.733
were the ones that folded cleanly.

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:15.210
The team at JEM doesn't go all the way

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:17.640
with the scoring process for every job.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.070
Take this bus crash in "Shang-Chi."

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:23.010
This shot was filmed on
location in San Francisco,

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:25.380
and the parked cars
getting run over are real.

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:27.540
JEM prepped them for destruction.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:30.180
The cars were supposed to
get crushed in the bus' path,

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:32.010
but they also needed to be strong enough

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:33.810
to slow down the bus.

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:36.630
So JEM only scored the upper
portion of the vehicles

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:40.020
to collapse on impact, keeping
the lower portions strong.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:43.620
When the bus would carry over
the tops of the vehicles,

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:46.080
if it went down too far, the
scene wouldn't look right.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:49.050
So all we wanted is
just so much compression

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:51.840
and actually provide a flat platform

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.540
for the camera to track
down the hill with.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:55.560
Narrator: They can also just focus

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:57.540
on a few major points of weakening,

00:02:57.540 --> 00:02:59.730
like these hinges on car doors.

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:02.490
Swapping out the steel
hinges for breakaway ones

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:05.340
makes the doors incredibly
easy to rip off.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:07.410
Cody: They're made out
of a weaker compound,

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:09.210
which makes them shear easier.

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:12.270
So that way, it'll snap
the door off nice and easy.

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:13.560
Narrator: Then they cut these wires

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.660
to make sure the door
has nothing to hang on to

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:17.430
after the bolts blow out.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:20.430
These weaker materials
allow for big results.

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:29.760
Some forms of damage can be faked,

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:32.310
like for scenes where an
actor crashes into something

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:34.683
and the hood of their
car dramatically pops up.

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:40.053
The team at JEM can rig
hoods to do that on cue.

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:42.930
Elia: So, that hood-popping rig,

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:45.960
basically we took a stock Crown Vic hood,

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:48.450
and we went inside it. We
actually scored it to a point.

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:50.190
So that way, when we put a cylinder on it

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:54.090
and we pulled it, it just
bent just like soft metal.

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:55.230
Narrator: They were able to do this

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:57.720
over and over again on the same hood,

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:00.450
reusing it through the
whole night of shooting.

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:03.170
The shop used a similar
technique to rig this truck

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:05.010
in "Ghostbusters: Afterlife."

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:06.810
Elia: And the storyline was that the ghost

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:09.630
is chasing this truck, and
he's punching at the truck.

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:11.760
Narrator: They had to make
the doors dent practically,

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:14.310
as if the ghost, who
would be added with CG,

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:16.290
was crashing into it.

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:19.140
To get the effect in camera,
Elia's team replicated

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:21.090
the door panels of the GMC pickup

00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:23.448
in a soft conditioned aluminum

00:04:23.448 --> 00:04:25.089
and attached the panels to a rig

00:04:25.089 --> 00:04:27.060
that made them buckle on cue.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:28.835
So that when we pulled
it with a pneumatic ram

00:04:28.835 --> 00:04:31.280
on the inside, it sucked it in,

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:34.363
and it looked like the ghost
was punching these panels.

00:04:36.944 --> 00:04:39.727
Narrator: The work isn't
limited to the car itself.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:44.370
JEM pulls all sorts of tricks on set

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:47.220
to make sure destruction
goes according to plan,

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:48.672
with ramps,

00:04:51.698 --> 00:04:52.831
cannons,

00:04:55.737 --> 00:04:56.790
and cables.

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:01.050
Elia: In our world, you
only get so many seconds

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:03.630
to tell the story, so they
want to make it dramatic.

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:06.570
Cars don't always just explode on impact.

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:08.612
Narrator: So JEM gives them a little help.

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:13.140
Elia: So what we'll do is use devices,

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:15.390
everything from a gas
bomb underneath a vehicle

00:05:15.390 --> 00:05:17.460
or to a, what's called a propane popper,

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:18.630
and attach it to the vehicle.

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:20.867
We'll have it fired,
whether it's on a wire,

00:05:20.867 --> 00:05:22.883
remote control, or an impact switch.

00:05:22.883 --> 00:05:24.674
Or sometimes even the stunt guy

00:05:24.674 --> 00:05:26.490
will have the button inside with him.

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:29.440
Narrator: To make cars go
flying at the press of a button,

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:33.300
the shop retrofits cars with
different types of cannons,

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:35.730
like on this test run, for
which the team was asked

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:37.505
to split a truck in half.

00:05:37.505 --> 00:05:40.838
They built a 10-inch dual-cannon
system to pull it off

00:05:40.838 --> 00:05:43.022
and used this system on a larger scale

00:05:43.022 --> 00:05:45.360
for this highway chase in "Taken 3."

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:46.950
Elia: We had to start with
a tractor trailer coming

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:50.100
down the highway, having to
jackknife [with] so much impact

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:53.310
that it caused the sea container
to fall off the trailer,

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:55.500
so much speed that when
it did hit the ground,

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:57.540
it rolled and ran over cars.

00:05:57.540 --> 00:05:58.500
Narrator: To execute the move,

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:01.290
they used two 12-inch nitrogen cannons,

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:02.910
which provided enough inertia

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:05.040
to flip the trailer's shipping container.

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:06.630
And for the rolling part?

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:07.993
Elia: We welded wheels on the end

00:06:07.993 --> 00:06:09.765
of the square sea container,

00:06:09.765 --> 00:06:12.000
so we could roll it down the highway.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:14.940
Narrator: A carefully placed
ramp can also make cars flip

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:17.174
in ways they would never do on their own.

00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:23.340
Elia: So when they come
in and they hit that ramp,

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:24.848
then the angle, the kicker at the end

00:06:24.848 --> 00:06:27.300
is what will send the car spiraling

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:29.580
and flipping over at speed.

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:31.680
Narrator: Productions hide
ramps on the blind side

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:35.073
of cars and also use set
decoration like roadblocks.

00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:39.910
When a destruction scene calls for a car

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:42.401
to follow a very specific trajectory,

00:06:42.401 --> 00:06:44.610
the job often involves cable rigs,

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:46.740
which can make the cars crash or spin

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:48.457
in a predetermined fashion.

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:53.850
JEM builds cable accelerators

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:57.292
to pull heavy vehicles in
a desired direction fast.

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:04.620
And limiting or suspender cables make cars

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:07.530
come to a full halt within a split second,

00:07:07.530 --> 00:07:10.680
something you can't do
with regular car brakes.

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:12.720
They used these cables in "Bright."

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:15.840
Elia: David Ayer wanted us
to have a Suburban come in,

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:18.810
hit this imaginary wall,
and stop on a dime.

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:21.510
And it was all shot very, very stylistic,

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:22.897
so you had a side profile,

00:07:22.897 --> 00:07:24.930
and it picks up the crumpling of the hood.

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:27.270
So what we did is, we
did a pull cable forward,

00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:29.100
but we also had a stop cable going

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:30.750
to the front of nose of the bumper.

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:33.390
We actually scored and weakened the front,

00:07:33.390 --> 00:07:36.030
so we collapsed it back
and held the vehicle

00:07:36.030 --> 00:07:38.400
from crossing that imaginary barrier.

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:40.237
Narrator: Cables also helped JEM execute

00:07:40.237 --> 00:07:42.620
one of its most customized crash jobs

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:44.094
for "Velvet Buzzsaw."

00:07:44.094 --> 00:07:47.370
The truck had to slam into
the corner of this building.

00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:48.330
That was a real building,

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:50.040
so we couldn't hurt the real building.

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:51.570
Narrator: And the car could only get hit

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:53.910
in a precise area on the passenger door,

00:07:53.910 --> 00:07:57.270
so that the audience would
believe the driver survived.

00:07:57.270 --> 00:08:00.300
To pull it off, JEM built
a whole new set element.

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:02.640
The team structurally embedded a fake pole

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:05.318
that was strong enough to
absorb the impact of the car ...

00:08:05.318 --> 00:08:07.007
Elia: A fake pole that's
in there hard enough

00:08:07.007 --> 00:08:10.110
that it didn't move and give
away that it was a fake pole.

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:12.328
Narrator: And over the car
doors placed an outer layer

00:08:12.328 --> 00:08:14.700
made from soft conditioned aluminum.

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:17.970
This would help them show
more impact with less.

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:20.700
For a crash that might
look random and chaotic,

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:24.270
JEM's team will spend hours
testing and experimenting

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:27.690
to make sure everything happens
exactly how they want it to.

00:08:27.690 --> 00:08:31.560
And even if it isn't realistic,
it sure looks like it.

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:34.380
Everything we do defies
physics. None of it makes sense.

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:36.038
But we get paid to do it.

